-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+Use 2Q cache replacement to protect comparison clips during large sequential screenings. Entries are split between two LRU queues:
+
+- **Inqueue (cold)**: new entries and entries accessed only once. Target size 30% of cache.
+- **Outqueue (hot)**: entries accessed twice or more. Target size 70% of cache.
+
+On a cache hit:
+- An entry in Inqueue moves to Outqueue on second access.
+- An entry in Outqueue is refreshed in place (moved to the tail of Outqueue).
+
+On admission (cache miss + successful render), the new entry is added to Inqueue. If this exceeds the 12 GiB limit:
+
+1. Evict LRU entries from Inqueue until the queue shrinks to its target size, or until cache size is within budget.
+2. If Inqueue is at target but total cache still exceeds budget, evict LRU entries from Outqueue.
+
+**Rationale**: Inqueue acts as a filter for one-off entries seen during sequential screening; Outqueue preserves clips accessed multiple times across sessions. This separation protects repeated comparison work from eviction by large unrelated screenings, which LRU alone would not.
+
+**Parameters**: Inqueue targets 30% of 12 GiB (3.6 GiB); Outqueue targets 70% (8.4 GiB). These targets are approximate — eviction is trigger-based and the split may drift within a session, but rebalance naturally as cache turns over.
